WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:05.390
Over the past four decades, Star Wars has
spawned plenty of movies, animated shows,

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.639
and video games, but live-action television
has always been the one unconquered frontier

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.050
in the Star Wars universe.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:13.540
At least, until now.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:20.100
At Star Wars Celebration 2019, showrunner
and creator Jon Favreau repeatedly emphasized

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.910
that The Mandalorian will be one giant love
letter to the original Star Wars trilogy,

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:27.560
and that tribute extends behind the scenes
as well.

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.600
Favreau wants to make sure The Mandalorian
feels like our first visit to that galaxy

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:33.680
far, far away.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:39.060
Naturally, green screens and CGI are necessary
to deliver The Mandalorian on a TV budget,

00:00:39.071 --> 00:00:43.850
but Favreau says that the crew is also using
puppets, animatronics, and special effects

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.500
makeup wherever possible.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.800
For example, while the dogfights in the modern
Star Wars films are mostly computer generated,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:55.760
Favreau really wanted to make a model of the
Razor Crest to use during production.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:01.040
Well, Industrial Light &amp; Magic's art staff
were so excited about the idea that they didn't

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.260
just make a model.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.680
Unprompted, they went home and built a camera
rig in their garages, recreating the hardware

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.439
that was used to make Star Wars back in 1977.

00:01:11.439 --> 00:01:14.670
The end result looks just like classic Star
Wars films.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.069
"You'll see, this is...isn't that cool?

00:01:18.069 --> 00:01:20.280
It already feels like Luke's X-Wing, right?"

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:25.320
ILM's staff aren't the only Star Wars fans
who got in on The Mandalorian action, either.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:30.600
The show's producers reached out to the 501st
Legion, a group of Star Wars fans who make

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.620
their own, highly detailed stormtrooper outfits.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:39.100
Lucasfilm has teamed with the 501st before
for promotional events, but The Mandalorian

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:44.260
marks the first time that the 501st has appeared
onscreen in an official Star Wars production.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:50.260
All of Star Wars owes a huge debt to George
Lucas after all, he created the thing.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:53.960
But The Mandalorian has some specific ties
to Star Wars' maker.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.560
For one, the show is heavily inspired by the
first Star Wars movie, especially the opening

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.060
act and the cantina scene.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:07.649
However, as Giancarlo Esposito has revealed,
Lucas' input on the series goes a lot deeper

00:02:07.649 --> 00:02:08.260
than that.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:12.820
According to Esposito, Lucas actually contributed
some creative ideas to the show.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:18.190
While discussing The Mandalorian with Collider,
Esposito said that Jon Favreau, quote, "figured

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:23.390
it out with George Lucas" himself, and worked
directly with him to make sure the show felt

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:24.620
like classic Star Wars.

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:30.591
Star Wars and video games have always enjoyed
a healthy relationship, and The Mandalorian

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.220
pushes it to the next level.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.280
While Jon Favreau and his team used practical
effects as much as they could to capture the

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:42.820
original Star Wars' hand-made charm, digital
wizardry was key to bringing The Mandalorian's

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.380
exotic worlds to life.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.100
But how did Favreau and the actors know what
their fictional locations were supposed to

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:49.600
look like?

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:51.680
By using a game engine, of course.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.920
Video game software is great at rendering
detailed 3D environments in real time, and

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:01.400
The Mandalorian team used that tech to create
digital sets that the cast and crew could

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.980
"film" and see the results in real time.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.430
Not only did that speed up production, but
it let The Mandalorian's creative minds get

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:12.400
an excellent read on what the finished product
would look like and adjust accordingly.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.380
The results should speak for themselves.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:20.160
Thanks to Boba Fett, we knew what a Mandalorian
looked like decades before we knew anything

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.880
about them as a people, and The Mandalorian
wouldn't be the same without him.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:29.300
Boba Fett debuted during the Star Wars Holiday
Special, and while the rest of the program

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:35.340
isn't worth your time, Nelvana Studios' animated
short "The Faithful Wookiee" absolutely is.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:40.500
Not only does the sequence make Fett cooler
than the movies ever did, but the design ended

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.820
up influencing both how the Mandalorians looked
on The Clone Wars.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.680
It also set the standard for The Mandalorian's
costume.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:51.980
That narrow visor, the big ol' holster, the
natural color scheme, and the visible battle

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:52.480
damage?

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.560
Those are all from his old short.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:58.440
It also gives an idea as to what The Mandalorian
might look like.

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:02.840
In fact, Nelvana's animators looked to westerns
when storyboarding the short.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:08.010
"The Faithful Wookiee's" surreal landscapes
and the dinosaur that Boba rides might be

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:13.160
a little too fanciful for live-action TV,
but if you want to see Star Wars reimagined

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.100
as a spaghetti western, you don't have to
wait for The Mandalorian's premiere.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:19.760
There's already an example out in the wild.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:24.860
If you're worried that creator Jon Favreau
isn't up to the task of bringing Star Wars

00:04:24.860 --> 00:04:26.400
to the small screen, don't be.

00:04:26.520 --> 00:04:27.300
He's got some help.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:32.460
Longtime Lucasfilm president Kathleen Kennedy
is listed as one of the show's producers,

00:04:32.469 --> 00:04:37.180
and all of the material will still go through
Lucasfilm's story group, the organization

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.880
in charge of keeping Star Wars canon consistent.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.940
Favreau's also getting a big, big hand from
the animated side of things.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:48.560
Dave Filoni may not be a household name, but
if you're a modern day Star Wars fan, you

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:49.180
know his work.

00:04:49.500 --> 00:04:54.080
Filoni directed The Clone Wars feature film,
and he worked side-by-side with George Lucas

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.640
to bring the subsequent animated series to
the air.

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:01.180
Filoni also co-created both Rebels and the
newest Star Wars cartoon, Resistance.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:07.120
In other words, he's the guy behind a lot
of the best of parts modern Star Wars canon.

00:05:07.249 --> 00:05:12.740
According to The Art of Star Wars: The Clone
Wars, Filoni is the brains behind Anakin Skywalker's

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:15.080
beloved apprentice, Ahsoka Tano.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:19.809
On Rebels, Filoni and his staff raised questions
about the Jedi's relationship with the Force

00:05:19.809 --> 00:05:23.340
years before Rian Johnson and The Last Jedi
did the same.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.140
"Revenge is not the Jedi way.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:29.800
I am no Jedi"

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:33.940
Heck, he even voiced Chopper, Rebels' scene-stealing
droid.

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:38.939
Filoni is serving as one of the show's executive
producers, and he'll make his live-action

00:05:38.939 --> 00:05:41.960
directing debut by helming The Mandalorian's
very first episode.

00:05:43.139 --> 00:05:48.430
The Mandalorian takes place about five years
after Return of the Jedi and 25 years before

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:53.160
The Force Awakens, making the galaxy a very
different place than what you're used to.

00:05:53.590 --> 00:05:58.800
The ragtag Rebel Alliance has transformed
itself into the New Republic, a representative

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:01.380
democracy that spans many known planets.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:04.840
The Galactic Empire, on the other hand, hasn't
been as fortunate.

00:06:05.000 --> 00:06:10.159
After the Battle of Jakku brought the Galactic
Civil War to a close, what remained of the

00:06:10.159 --> 00:06:14.949
main Imperial fleet retreated to the mysterious
Unknown Regions, where it will eventually

00:06:14.949 --> 00:06:16.680
reform into the First Order.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:17.820
Not yet, though.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:19.419
The First Order is still years away.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:20.840
It's a time of transition.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:26.060
Out on the Outer Rim, where The Mandalorian
takes place, former Imperial warlords are

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.980
scrambling to hold on to power.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:31.800
Former rebels are trying to figure out where
they belong now that the fighting has stopped.

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:36.060
Criminals try to profit off of the confusion,
and The Mandalorian's main characters are

00:06:36.060 --> 00:06:37.720
all caught right in the middle of it.

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:43.000
The Mandalorian isn't even out yet, and it's
already a hit or, at the very least, Disney

00:06:43.000 --> 00:06:45.180
seems extremely confident that it'll be one.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:49.460
Months before The Mandalorian debuted, Jon
Favreau got the greenlight to start writing

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:50.460
season two.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:54.520
By the time that the cast and crew started
making the rounds to promote the first season,

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.140
filming on the second was already underway.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:00.180
Season two adds a couple of new directors
to its already stacked lineup.

00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:04.110
For one, Favreau will actually get to helm
an episode or two of the series this time

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:04.600
around.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:09.040
During season one, he was too busy on Disney's
The Lion King to take a direct role behind

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:09.540
the camera.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:13.440
The Mandalorian cast member Carl Weathers
will also direct a season two installment.

00:07:13.450 --> 00:07:18.319
In fact, his interest in sitting in the director's
chair was one of the main reasons why he joined

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:18.820
the series.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:21.580
"Dreams are worth fighting for.

00:07:21.760 --> 00:07:25.840
Now are you going to be a fighter, or are
you gonna be a doctor?"

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.400
"You're right, Carl Weathers."

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:32.980
Favreau promises that there are plenty of
surprises and a few old "Legends" characters

00:07:32.980 --> 00:07:33.600
in the pipeline.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:36.800
Don't give up on The Mandalorian when season
one ends in December.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:38.860
The show is just getting started.

